WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.342
Attention can compare tokens, but the model still needs information about sequence order.

00:00:05.342 --> 00:00:11.117
Compare “the student submitted the assignment” with “the assignment submitted the student.” The important

00:00:11.117 --> 00:00:16.727
words are nearly identical, yet their positions create very different meanings.

00:00:16.727 --> 00:00:23.737
Transformer architectures therefore add or encode position information along with token representations.

00:00:23.737 --> 00:00:28.127
The original Transformer used sinusoidal positional encodings.

00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:31.237
Many models use learned position embeddings.

00:00:31.237 --> 00:00:37.082
Newer architectures may use relative position methods or rotary position embeddings.

00:00:37.082 --> 00:00:42.662
The implementation differs, but the purpose is the same: the model needs a signal telling it where tokens

00:00:42.662 --> 00:00:48.777
occur and, in many designs, how tokens are positioned relative to one another.

00:00:48.777 --> 00:00:54.307
For this course, focus on the role rather than memorizing every positional method.

00:00:54.307 --> 00:01:00.422
Token identity tells the model what is present; positional information helps tell it where that information

00:01:00.422 --> 00:01:00.960
occurs.
